import{_ as a,H as t,f as r,i as n}from"./chunks/framework.CGh9YNlq.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"1.Adapter Tuning","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"notes/PEFT.md","filePath":"notes/PEFT.md"}'),s={name:"notes/PEFT.md"};function i(o,e,p,d,l,m){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<p>The goal is to adapt large language models by training only a small subset of parameters instead of updating the entire model.</p><h1 id="_1-adapter-tuning" tabindex="-1">1.Adapter Tuning <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-adapter-tuning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.Adapter Tuning&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Adapter Tuning inserts a small neural network(an adapter)into each Transformer block.</p><p>For example:</p><p>Transformer Block</p><p>Attention |<br> Adapter | Layer Norm consider bottleneck dimension is 738-&gt;32-&gt;738 | 738<em>32+32</em>738~50K parameters &lt; 738*738 ps Adapter | Layer Norm</p><p>Advantages: Easy to implement; Well suited for multi-task learning; One base model can host many adapters, switching tasks simply requires loading a different adapter.</p><p>e.g. --Language Adapter --Mathematics Adapter --Medical Adapter --Finance Adapter</p><h1 id="lora" tabindex="-1">LoRA <a class="header-anchor" href="#lora" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LoRA&quot;">​</a></h1><p>LoRA takes a completely different approach. Instead of adding layers, it modifies the weight update.</p><p>For example:</p><p>Hiden size = 4096 rank = 8</p><p>*w=BA A: r * d B: d * r</p><p>then the trainable parameters become 4096<em>8+8</em>4096~65K parameters &lt;4096*4096 ps A and B are optimized.</p><hr><p>Memory Usage: Full Fine-turning requires storing Model parameters, Gradients,Optimizer states, which typically consums hundreds of GB of GPU memory. LoRA, since the base model is frozen, only the LoRA parameters require gradients and optimizer states.GPU memory usage is often reduced to 10% or less compared with full fine-tuning. Adapter Tuning also reduces momory significantly, althhough it typically comsumes slightly more memory than LoRA.</p><hr><p>Multi-Task Deployment:</p><p>For Adapter Tuning, switching tasks only requires loading a different adapter. LoRA is similar, modern inference frameworks even support stacking multiple LoRA modules.</p><hr><p>Summary:</p><p>LoRA learns low-rank updates to existing weight matrices. It requires fewer trainable parameters, can be merged into the base model, and has virtually no inference overhead. LoRA has become the dominant PEFT method for modern open-source LLMs, because it trains fewer parameters, it achieves performance close to full fine-tuning for many tasks, it can be merged into the base model for efficient deployment, it is the default PEFT method supported bu Hugging Face PEFT, OLoRA, and many open-source training frameworks.</p><p>Adapter Tuning inserts small trainable neural network modules into Transformer blocks. It offers strong modularitu but introduces a small inference overhead. Adapter Tuning remains valuable in scenarios: A single base model must support many independent task-specific modules. Multi-tenant systems require fast switching between task adapters. Researchers want modular and composable task-specific components.</p><p>For superviesd fine-tuning(SFT) of modern open-source LLMs, LoRA is generally the preferred approach. Adapter Tuning remains a good choice when maintaining many independently switchable task modules is a priority.</p>',24)])])}const g=a(s,[["render",i]]);export{c as __pageData,g as default};
